<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Namnetes</h1><p class="st-meta">300 BCE – 56 BCE (approx.) · capital Condevicnum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Gallic people of the Loire estuary, allies of the Veneti against Caesar, who gave their name to the city of Nantes.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Namnetes lived on the north bank of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, with a port called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Condevicnum</a> or Portus Namnetum on the site of Nantes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> and Ptolemy place them between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/veneti">Veneti</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pictones">Pictones</a>, and Greek writers knew the Loire mouth as a trading point for Atlantic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, linked to the earlier port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corbilo">Corbilo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Little is known of their government. They took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a> of the Armorican peoples and struck gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coins of Armorican style.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 56 BCE the Namnetes sent help to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/veneti">Veneti</a> in their naval war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Caesar</a> and shared in their defeat. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> their port grew into the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Condevicnum</a>, renamed after the people as Namnetes, the origin of the name Nantes.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/namnetes.jpg" alt="Namnetes" width="320" height="166"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coins_of_the_Namnetes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 BCE – 56 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Condevicnum</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gaulish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-178&amp;focus=namnetes" aria-label="Namnetes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4753 560 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Namnetes lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5073 816h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5073 816h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day France. <a href="/atlas?year=-178&amp;focus=namnetes">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

